# priv_ops — RiftNest private operations

Helper scripts for giving persistent RiftNest instances their own
routable IPs + SSH access. Reachable over VPN only (10.0.2.x clients).

## IP plan (all /24, gateway always x.x.x.1 on br0)

| Range       | Purpose              | Gateway  |
|-------------|----------------------|----------|
| 10.0.2.0/24 | VPN clients          | 10.0.2.1 (tun0) |
| 10.0.3.0/24 | Containers (pool A)  | 10.0.3.1 (br0)  |
| 10.0.4.0/24 | Containers (pool B)  | 10.0.4.1 (br0)  |
| 10.0.5.0/24 | Containers (pool C)  | 10.0.5.1 (br0)  |
| 10.0.6.0/24 | Containers (pool D)  | 10.0.6.1 (br0)  |
| 10.0.7.0/24 | Containers (pool E)  | 10.0.7.1 (br0)  |
| 10.0.8.0/24 | Containers (pool F)  | 10.0.8.1 (br0)  |
| 10.0.9.0/24 | Containers (pool G)  | 10.0.9.1 (br0)  |
| 10.0.10.0/24 | Containers (pool H) | 10.0.10.1 (br0) |
| 10.0.11.0/24 | Containers (pool I) | 10.0.11.1 (br0) |
| 10.0.12.0/24 | Containers (pool J) | 10.0.12.1 (br0) |

`.4.x` through `.12.x` are extra container pools (sometimes called VMs —
same thing here). Pick the next free address in the range you want.

## mybox-netns.sh

Attaches a persistent instance to br0 with its own IP and starts
dropbear (SSH) inside it under proot in that network namespace.

Live copy: `/root/mybox-netns.sh` (this dir holds a reference copy).

Usage:
  bash /root/mybox-netns.sh <name> <ip>

Examples:
  bash /root/mybox-netns.sh apidemo 10.0.3.3   # container range
  bash /root/mybox-netns.sh webvm  10.0.4.2    # VM range

What it does:
  1. Kills any stale holder/veth for <name>
  2. Creates a persistent network namespace (sleep-infinity holder)
  3. Creates veth pair, plugs host end into br0
  4. Assigns <ip>/24 + default route via x.x.x.1 inside the namespace
  5. Starts dropbear under proot rooted at the instance dir, port 22

Prerequisites (per instance, one time, via riftnest CLI):
  riftnest create <name> <image>
  riftnest exec <name> /sbin/apk add dropbear
  riftnest exec <name> /bin/sh -c "mkdir -p /etc/dropbear && /usr/bin/dropbearkey -t rsa -f /etc/dropbear/dropbear_rsa_host_key"
  riftnest exec <name> /bin/sh -c "echo root:<password> | chpasswd"

Then connect (VPN up):
  ssh root@<ip>

## Notes
- Namespaces die on reboot — rerun the script per instance after reboot
  (or wire it into a systemd unit later).
- br0 carries all three ranges; secondary addresses .4.1/.5.1 are
  configured by riftnet-netinit.sh on boot.
- OpenVPN pushes routes for all three ranges; reconnect VPN clients
  after changing pushed routes.
